  note: a parameter written without `@` is borrowed: the caller keeps the value and reads it again after the call, so a call that frees it leaves them holding an address C has taken back. Write the parameter `@o` in the signature of `closed_field` and `@` before its argument at every call of `closed_field`, which says on both lines that the value does not survive; this call stays as it is, since `g5_close` takes this argument without `@`
  note: this refuses the call, not the copy: a copy made BEFORE the call still holds the freed address, and nothing here sees that. design.md Part 8 wart 20 states that cost and names what would close it
